#!/usr/bin/env python3
# ryuji-fan-guard v2.7: keep ryuji's BMC fan offset at Rohit's quiet -80 at idle and under every load (v2.7: no light-load
# step any more; v2.4-v2.6 used -60, v2.3 -40), go to a moderate -16 only when the board or the CPUs run hot, and use
# Performance / Full only if -16 is not enough. The BMC's own curves keep running underneath (the offset shifts both the CPU and the system fan curves).
# Measured 2026-10-06 (room ~27C; ryuji-profile sweeps, STATUS-LOG.md):
#   offset  idle CPU/sys fans   SIO Temp 1 idle   4 busy threads: CPU/sys fans, SIO1  full load: CPU/sys fans, SIO1
#   -80     ~2,300 / 1,050       77-78C            3,770 / 1,050, climbs past 79       4,890 / 3,175, 55C (CPU 70C)
#   -16     ~4,050 / 1,260       ~71.5C            4,940 / 3,325, 54C                  5,730 / 4,815, 46C
#     0     ~4,500 / 2,250       ~59-60C           5,300 / 4,065, 48C                  5,990 / 5,350, 44C
# SIO Temp 1 (BMC warns at 80, critical 85) follows system-fan airflow: it falls within ~3 min when they speed up and
# rises with a ~10 min time constant at the -80 floor. At -80 the system fans stay at their floor until the CPUs
# reach ~65-70C, so light-to-moderate load in a warm room lets SIO1 creep past 79 and v1 (-80 -> Performance)
# flipped between quiet and loud. Full load is fine at -80 (the BMC curve raises the system fans itself), so v2
# only leaves -80 when SIO1 is actually climbing near its limit, not on CPU load alone.
# Levels, quietest first: -80 (QUIET), -16 (WARM), 0 (Performance), 127 (Full).
#   QUIET -> WARM: SIO1 >= SIO_UP for 2 readings ('hot'), or SIO1 >= SIO_CLIMB, rising >= 1C over 5 min while the
#                  CPUs are busy (>= LOAD_UP) for 2 readings ('load'), or CPU >= CPU_UP / VR >= VR_UP / DIMM >= DIMM_UP
#                  for 2 readings ('load').
#   WARM -> QUIET: idle (CPU <= LOAD_DOWN, VR/DIMM cool) for 10 min and SIO1 <= SIO_BACK_LOAD (if load put us
#                  there) / SIO1 <= SIO_BACK_HOT for 30 min (if the board was hot, i.e. a warm room), so QUIET does
#                  not climb straight back to SIO_UP. v2.1 (2026-10-06 14:00): SIO_BACK_HOT 68 -> 71, so a hot-room trip
#                  ends when the room cools a little instead of holding -16 until the room is ~23C.
#   WARM -> Performance: SIO1 >= SIO_UP for 6 readings at WARM, or CPU >= CPU_UP / VR >= VR_UP / DIMM >= DIMM_UP for 2.
#   Performance -> WARM after 30 min with SIO1 <= 73 and CPU/VR/DIMM below their UP limits minus a margin.
#   Full at SIO1 >= 83 or CPU >= 86; back to Performance after 5 min below.
#   (v2.3 adds BUSY between QUIET and WARM for the 'load' case; see the v2.3 note below.)
# Runs as root on ryuji; the BMC password is in PWFILE (0600). Stop the service to set the offset by hand.
# v2.2 (2026-10-08): at boot the guard starts right after network-online.target, but ryuji's address is not usable yet
# (seen at both boots on 10-06 and 10-07: "Network is unreachable"), so the first BMC read is retried every 10 s for
# up to 2 min before assuming QUIET. The unit also gets Wants=network-online.target.
# v2.3 (2026-10-10): a BUSY level (-40) for a sustained light load. Measured 10-10 (room 24C, ryuji-floor.py plan psu:
# 30 min holds at 4 busy threads, -80/-40/-40/-80): at -80 SIO1 climbs 71 -> 74 (5 min) -> 77 (13-18 min) -> 78 and the
# power-supply fan's tone climbs into the 680-830 Hz band the rack rings at (768-782 Hz in 86-93% of the clips); v2.2
# then stepped to WARM (-16: CPU ~4,900 / system ~3,300 rpm under that load). At -40 (CPU 4,300 / system 1,900 rpm)
# SIO1 holds 59-61C, the tone stays at 586-636 Hz (0-1% of the clips in the band) and the mic reads 0.7 dB(A) less
# than at -80. So:
#   QUIET -> BUSY:  SIO1 >= SIO_BUSY (74) and climbing (>= 1C over 5 min) while the CPUs are busy, 2 readings: about
#                   5-6 min into a light load, before the PSU tone reaches the ringing band. Full load still stays at
#                   -80 (SIO1 falls there: the BMC raises the system fans itself). Idle never triggers it.
#   BUSY -> WARM:   SIO1 still climbing past SIO_CLIMB (77) under load, SIO1 >= SIO_UP, or CPU/VR/DIMM limits.
#   QUIET -> WARM:  unchanged for a hot board at idle (-40 does not lift the system fans at idle; -16 does) and for the
#                   CPU/VR/DIMM limits.
#   BUSY -> QUIET:  idle for 10 min with SIO1 <= SIO_BACK_BUSY (77; QUIET idles ~1C above BUSY, so it stays under
#                   SIO_UP). At idle -40 costs ~0.2 dB(A) over -80 (10-08 sweep), so a long hold there is harmless.
#   Idle now means fewer than IDLE_THREADS logical CPUs busy (/proc/stat) instead of the hottest CPU <= LOAD_DOWN: at
#   -40 the CPU fans cool the CPUs under a light load (57C at room 24C, ~3C less per 3C cooler room), so the temperature
#   rule could read a light load in a cool room as idle and step down mid-job. VR/DIMM must still be cool. If
#   /proc/stat cannot be read, the v2.2 temperature rule is used.
# v2.4 (2026-10-13), for the 2x Xeon E5-2696 v4 fitted 2026-10-12 (22 cores each, 150 W PL1; were 2x E5-2650 v3).
# Measured 10-12 evening (ryuji out of the rack, room 24-25C, 4 busy threads, settled 15 min of 25-30 min holds,
# A/B/A/C, scripts/ryuji-floor.py plans v4light/v4b/v4a2/v4c):
#   offset  CPU / system fans    SIO1  hottest CPU  dB(A)   PSU tone
#   -80     4,200 / 1,300-1,600  64-68  63-64       -67.0   782 Hz, in the 680-830 Hz band the rack/room rings at
#   -60     4,500 / 2,100-2,600  54     61          -67.0   662-665 Hz, under the band
#   -40     4,700 / 2,750-2,950  48     56-57       -66.0   601-615 Hz
# On these CPUs the BMC's own curve lifts the system fans under a light load even at -80, so SIO1 FALLS (72 -> 64-68C)
# instead of climbing, and v2.3's QUIET -> BUSY rule (SIO1 >= 74 and climbing) never fired (30 min at 4 threads, -80
# throughout, the PSU whine in the band from minute 5). And -40 now costs 1 dB(A) of fan noise; -60 is as quiet as -80
# without the whine. So:
#   BUSY = -60 (was -40).
#   QUIET -> BUSY: at least BUSY_THREADS logical CPUs busy (/proc/stat) for 2 readings (~1-2 min into a job; the PSU
#                  tone reaches the band ~5 min in at -80). The SIO1 rule stays only as the fallback when /proc/stat
#                  cannot be read. Full load also goes to BUSY first (more air for CPUs that now draw 150 W each).
#   CPU_EMERG 86 -> 83 (Full Speed): the new CPUs' limits are lower (BMC: 82 upper non-critical, 85 critical; coretemp
#                  high 83, crit 85).
#   Everything else as in v2.3 (BUSY -> QUIET after 10 idle min with SIO1 <= 77; BUSY -> WARM on SIO1 or CPU/VR/DIMM).
# v2.5 (2026-10-13): full-load profile (fixed offsets, 8 min phases, room 24C, ryuji out of the rack; package W = both
# CPUs' RAPL counters, wall W = the BMC's DCMI reading):
#   workers  package W  wall W  offset  hottest CPU  CPU / system fans   dB(A)    PSU tone
#   22       188        368-370 -60     64           4,800 / 2,975       -65.47   442 Hz
#   22       188        368-370 -80     66           4,600 / 2,525       -65.09   729 Hz, in the band
#   33       245        457     -60     68-69        5,700-5,900 / 4,925-5,125  -57.27  817-820 Hz (A/B/A mean)
#   33       246        457     -80     70           5,700 / 4,750       -58.21   820 Hz
#   44       296        534-538 -60     72           6,500 / 6,225       -50.30   920 Hz
#   44       296        534-538 -80     74           6,300 / 5,900       -53.01   899 Hz
#   (44 at -16: 69C, -48.30; at Performance: 69C, -45.97; no thermal throttling at any offset.)
# What decides it is the power supply: its fan's tone rises with its load and crosses the ringing band between ~370 and
# ~530 W at the wall (at the band's top edge at 457 W). Below ~400 W, -60 keeps the tone down (v2.4); above it the tone
# is at or past the band's top at any offset, the
# BMC's own curve already drives the fans hard, and -60 only adds fan noise (2.7 dB(A) at full load for 2C). The CPU
# package power (RAPL) tracks the PSU load whatever the room or the job, so it is the trigger, not threads or temps.
#   QUIET -> BUSY:  busy threads as in v2.4, and package power under BUSY_PKG_MAX (215 W; 22 workers draw 188 W).
#   The crossover sits between 188 W (-60 better: 0.4 dB(A) and no whine) and 245 W (-80 better by ~1 dB(A)).
#   BUSY -> QUIET:  also at HEAVY_PKG or more for HEAVY_READINGS readings in a row (~5 min). Between the two nothing
#                   changes, and the 5 min keep a job whose power swings across the line every few minutes at -60
#                   instead of stepping with it (the harness's case 26 stepped every 3 min with 2 readings).
#   A full load from idle stays at -80 (the package power passes BUSY_PKG_MAX within a reading); CPU_UP (78) still takes
#   it to -16 in a warm room. Without the RAPL counters v2.4's rule applies.
# v2.6 (2026-10-13 16:10): the same logic, thresholds 215 / 235 W -> 160 / 180 W. The v2.5 numbers came from an 8 min hold
# at 22 workers that caught the PSU before it had warmed (-60: tone 442 Hz). 20 min holds, A/B/A, load held through
# (ryuji-floor.py plan v4half, last 10 min): -80 -64.47 / -60 -64.00 / -80 -64.41 dB(A), the tone at 735-768 Hz in the band
# at both, 500 Hz octave 1.5 dB higher at -60. So -60 helps only for a light load (2-4 threads, 90-110 W: same dB(A) or
# 0.75 dB less, ~2 dB less whine at 4 threads); from 22 workers (188 W) up, -80 is as quiet or quieter. 11 workers
# (148 W) measured only at -60 (tone 665 Hz after 8 min), so it stays on the -60 side of the line.
# v2.7 (2026-10-14): ryuji is back in the rack (on rails) and the mic sits ~4 ft out on the mattress. 20 min
# holds, A/B/A, load held through (ryuji-floor.py plan fin_light, last 10 min, room 23-24C, idle floor -69.7 dB(A)):
#   2 threads:  -80 +2.3 / -60 +4.3 / -80 +2.3 dB(A); strongest tone 750 Hz at -80 dBFS (-80) vs 636 Hz at -73 (-60)
#   4 threads:  -60 +4.9 / -80 +4.2 / -40 +5.8 / -60 +4.1 dB(A)
#   11 workers: -60 +6.3 / -80 +3.9 (last 10 min) / -40 +9.1 (first 3 min; cut short)
# In the rack the extra system-fan speed costs more than the PSU whine it moves out of the band, and Rohit, listening
# from the living room, called the -40 step "very noticable ... could not call this white noise" while -80 at 11
# workers went unnoticed. So the guard holds -80 under any load. Kept: -16 when SIO1 climbs past SIO_CLIMB under load
# (v2.2's rule, now straight from -80) or reaches SIO_UP, or a CPU/VR/DIMM limit; Performance / Full above that. BUSY
# stays a known level only so that a BMC left at -60 by v2.4-v2.6 is put back to -80 at once.
import json, re, ssl, subprocess, sys, time, urllib.parse, urllib.request, http.cookiejar

BMC, USER, PWFILE = 'https://10.0.0.118', 'root', '/root/.bmc-pass'
STATE = '/run/ryuji-fan-guard.state'
QUIET, BUSY, WARM, PERF, FULL = -80, -60, -16, 0, 127
LEVELS = (QUIET, BUSY, WARM, PERF, FULL)
SIO_UP = 79                      # SIO Temp 1: BMC warns at 80, critical at 85
SIO_BACK_LOAD, SIO_BACK_HOT = 72, 71   # v2.1: 71 (was 68): -80 idles ~6C above -16, so 71 at -16 = ~77 at -80, under SIO_UP
SIO_PERF_DOWN = 73
LOAD_UP, LOAD_DOWN = 52, 50      # hottest CPU: idle at -80 is 41-50C, 4 busy threads 54-61C
SIO_CLIMB = 77                   # SIO1 at or above this and climbing under load -> WARM before it reaches SIO_UP
SIO_BUSY, SIO_BACK_BUSY = 74, 77  # v2.3: climbing under load from 74 -> BUSY; BUSY -> QUIET at idle with SIO1 <= 77
IDLE_THREADS = 1.0               # v2.3: idle = less than one logical CPU's worth busy (ryuji idles at ~0.05 of 88)
BUSY_THREADS = 2.0               # v2.4: QUIET -> BUSY at this many busy logical CPUs (2 readings)
BUSY_PKG_MAX, HEAVY_PKG = 160, 180   # v2.6 (v2.5: 215, 235): package W (both CPUs): BUSY only under 160 W; QUIET at 180 W+
HEAVY_READINGS = 5                   # v2.5: ... for this many readings in a row (~5 min)
RAPL = ('/sys/class/powercap/intel-rapl:0', '/sys/class/powercap/intel-rapl:1')
CPU_UP, VR_UP, DIMM_UP = 78, 90, 75
VR_COOL, DIMM_COOL = 80, 65
SIO_EMERG, CPU_EMERG = 83, 83   # v2.4: CPU 86 -> 83 (E5-2696 v4: BMC critical 85)
HOLD = {'load': 600, 'hot': 1800, PERF: 1800, FULL: 300}   # seconds of cool readings before stepping down
INTERVAL, VERIFY = 60, 1800

def log(m):
    print(m, flush=True)

# --- BMC web API (Avocent MergePoint; same calls its Utilities page makes) ---
def opener():
    ctx = ssl.create_default_context()
    ctx.check_hostname, ctx.verify_mode = False, ssl.CERT_NONE
    ctx.set_ciphers('DEFAULT@SECLEVEL=0')
    return urllib.request.build_opener(urllib.request.HTTPSHandler(context=ctx),
                                       urllib.request.HTTPCookieProcessor(http.cookiejar.CookieJar()))

def esc(v):  # the login page's encodeSetValue()
    for a, b in [('\\', '\\\\'), (':', '\\:'), (',', '\\,'), ('(', '\\('), (')', '\\)'), ('=', '\\='), ('&', '\\&'), ('?', '\\?')]:
        v = v.replace(a, b)
    return urllib.parse.quote(v, safe='')

def bmc(action, offset=None):
    """action 'get' -> current offset; 'set' -> offset after setting (read back)."""
    op = opener()
    def req(path, data=None, st2=None):
        r = urllib.request.Request(BMC + path, data=data)
        if data is not None:
            r.add_header('Content-Type', 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded')
        if st2:
            r.add_header('ST2', st2)
        with op.open(r, timeout=20) as resp:
            return resp.read().decode(errors='replace')
    pw = open(PWFILE).read().strip('\n')
    if '<authResult>0</authResult>' not in req('/data/login', f'user={USER}&password={esc(pw)}'.encode()):
        raise RuntimeError('BMC login failed')
    m = re.search(r'CSRFHandler\(\s*"ST1",\s*"([^"]+)",\s*"ST2",\s*"([^"]+)"', req('/index.html'))
    st1, st2 = m.group(1), m.group(2)
    try:
        def current():
            raw = int(re.search(r'var fanCurve\s*=\s*(\d+);', req(f'/utilities.html?ST1={st1}', st2=st2)).group(1))
            return raw if raw < 128 else 128 - raw
        if action == 'set':
            raw = offset if offset >= 0 else 128 - offset
            req(f'/gbtdata?set=FanCurveSet(1,{raw})&ST1={st1}', st2=st2)
        return current()
    finally:
        try:
            req(f'/data/logout?ST1={st1}', st2=st2)
        except Exception:
            pass

# --- sensors (in-band IPMI) ---
def temps():
    out = subprocess.run(['ipmitool', 'sdr', 'type', 'Temperature'], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30).stdout
    t = {}
    for line in out.splitlines():
        f = [x.strip() for x in line.split('|')]
        m = re.match(r'(\d+) degrees', f[4]) if len(f) >= 5 else None
        if m:
            t[f[0]] = int(m.group(1))
    if 'SIO Temp 1' not in t:
        return None
    return {'sio': t['SIO Temp 1'],
            'cpu': max(v for k, v in t.items() if re.match(r'CPU\d_TEMP', k)),
            'vr': max([v for k, v in t.items() if k.startswith('VR_')] or [0]),
            'dimm': max([v for k, v in t.items() if k.startswith('DIMM')] or [0])}

_stat = None
def busy_threads():
    """logical CPUs busy since the last call (non-idle share of /proc/stat jiffies x CPU count); None on the first call"""
    global _stat
    with open('/proc/stat') as f:
        lines = f.read().splitlines()
    v = [int(x) for x in lines[0].split()[1:9]]     # user nice system idle iowait irq softirq steal
    n = sum(1 for line in lines if re.match(r'cpu\d', line))
    prev, _stat = _stat, v
    if prev is None:
        return None
    d = [a - b for a, b in zip(v, prev)]
    total = sum(d)
    return round((total - d[3] - d[4]) / total * n, 2) if total > 0 else None

_rapl = None
def pkg_watts():
    """v2.5: both CPU packages' power since the last call (RAPL energy counters, wrap-safe); None on the first call"""
    global _rapl
    now = time.monotonic()
    e = [int(open(f'{z}/energy_uj').read()) for z in RAPL]
    prev, _rapl = _rapl, (now, e)
    if prev is None or now - prev[0] <= 0:
        return None
    total = 0
    for z, a, b in zip(RAPL, prev[1], e):
        d = b - a
        if d < 0:
            d += int(open(f'{z}/max_energy_range_uj').read())
        total += d
    return round(total / 1e6 / (now - prev[0]), 1)

def wanted(level, t, hot, cool, now, why):
    """the level the current readings ask for; stepping down only after the HOLD time of cool readings.
    why = what put us at WARM ('load' or 'hot')."""
    if t['sio'] >= SIO_EMERG or t['cpu'] >= CPU_EMERG:
        return FULL
    if level == FULL:
        return PERF if now - cool.get('full', now) >= HOLD[FULL] else FULL
    if level >= WARM and (hot.get('perf_sio', 0) >= 6 or hot.get('perf_other', 0) >= 2):
        return max(level, PERF)     # SIO1 gets 6 min at WARM first: it settles with tau ~3.5 min
    if level == QUIET:
        if hot.get('perf_other', 0) >= 2 or hot.get('load', 0) >= 2:
            return WARM     # v2.7: a CPU/VR/DIMM limit, or SIO1 climbing past SIO_CLIMB under load (v2.2's rule)
        return WARM if hot.get('sio', 0) >= 2 else QUIET
    if level == BUSY:       # v2.7: only when the guard finds the BMC at -60 (left by v2.4-v2.6): back to -80 at once
        if hot.get('load', 0) >= 2 or hot.get('sio', 0) >= 2 or hot.get('perf_other', 0) >= 2:
            return WARM
        return QUIET
    if level == PERF:
        return WARM if now - cool.get('perf', now) >= HOLD[PERF] else PERF
    # WARM: back to QUIET only when idle and SIO1 is low enough that QUIET will not climb straight back
    if why == 'hot':
        return QUIET if now - cool.get('hot', now) >= HOLD['hot'] else WARM
    return QUIET if now - cool.get('load', now) >= HOLD['load'] else WARM

def main():
    level, last_verify, hot, cool, why, sio_hist = None, 0, {}, {}, 'load', []
    for attempt in range(13):   # the network can lag network-online.target at boot: retry for up to 2 min
        try:
            level = bmc('get')
            log(f'starting; BMC offset is {level}' + (f' (after {attempt * 10} s)' if attempt else ''))
            break
        except Exception as ex:
            if attempt == 12:
                log(f'starting; could not read the BMC offset for 2 min ({ex!r}); assuming {QUIET}')
                level = QUIET
            else:
                time.sleep(10)
    if level not in LEVELS:
        log(f'offset {level} is not one of the guard levels; treating it as {QUIET}')
        level = QUIET
    fails = 0
    while True:
        now = time.time()
        try:
            t = temps()
        except Exception:
            t = None
        if t is None:
            fails += 1
            if fails == 5 and level < PERF:
                log('temps unreadable for 5 minutes; going to Performance (0) to be safe')
                try:
                    level = bmc('set', PERF)
                except Exception as ex:
                    log(f'BMC set failed: {ex!r}')
            time.sleep(INTERVAL)
            continue
        fails = 0
        try:
            threads = busy_threads()
        except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
            threads = None
        try:
            pkg = pkg_watts()
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            pkg = None
        sio_hist.append(t['sio']); del sio_hist[:-6]          # last 6 readings (5 min)
        climbing = t['sio'] >= SIO_CLIMB and len(sio_hist) == 6 and t['sio'] - sio_hist[0] >= 1 and t['cpu'] >= LOAD_UP
        hot['load'] = hot.get('load', 0) + 1 if climbing else 0
        if threads is not None:      # v2.4: a sustained load by busy threads (SIO1 no longer climbs under load)
            busy = threads >= BUSY_THREADS and (pkg is None or pkg < BUSY_PKG_MAX)     # v2.5: not a heavy load
            hot['heavy'] = hot.get('heavy', 0) + 1 if threads >= BUSY_THREADS and pkg is not None and pkg >= HEAVY_PKG else 0
        else:                        # v2.3 rule as the fallback when /proc/stat cannot be read
            busy = t['sio'] >= SIO_BUSY and len(sio_hist) == 6 and t['sio'] - sio_hist[0] >= 1 and t['cpu'] >= LOAD_UP
        hot['busy'] = hot.get('busy', 0) + 1 if busy else 0
        hot['sio'] = hot.get('sio', 0) + 1 if t['sio'] >= SIO_UP else 0
        hot['perf_sio'] = hot.get('perf_sio', 0) + 1 if t['sio'] >= SIO_UP else 0
        hot['perf_other'] = hot.get('perf_other', 0) + 1 if (t['cpu'] >= CPU_UP or t['vr'] >= VR_UP
                                                           or t['dimm'] >= DIMM_UP) else 0
        idle = ((threads < IDLE_THREADS) if threads is not None else (t['cpu'] <= LOAD_DOWN)) \
            and t['vr'] <= VR_COOL and t['dimm'] <= DIMM_COOL
        for key, ok in (('load', idle and t['sio'] <= SIO_BACK_LOAD),
                        ('busy', idle and t['sio'] <= SIO_BACK_BUSY),
                        ('hot', idle and t['sio'] <= SIO_BACK_HOT),
                        ('perf', t['sio'] <= SIO_PERF_DOWN and t['cpu'] <= CPU_UP - 8 and t['vr'] <= VR_UP - 10
                         and t['dimm'] <= DIMM_UP - 10),
                        ('full', t['sio'] < SIO_EMERG - 2 and t['cpu'] < CPU_EMERG - 4)):
            if ok:
                cool.setdefault(key, now)
            else:
                cool.pop(key, None)
        want = wanted(level, t, hot, cool, now, why)
        if want != level:
            try:
                if level in (QUIET, BUSY) and want == WARM:
                    why = 'hot' if hot.get('sio', 0) >= 2 and hot.get('load', 0) < 2 else 'load'
                got = bmc('set', want)
                log(f'offset {level} -> {got} (sio={t["sio"]} cpu={t["cpu"]} vr={t["vr"]} dimm={t["dimm"]}'
                    f' threads={threads} pkg={pkg}{"; " + why if want == WARM and level in (QUIET, BUSY) else ""})')
                level, last_verify = got, now
                cool.clear(); hot.clear()   # every level starts its own counts
            except Exception as ex:
                log(f'BMC set to {want} failed: {ex!r}; retrying next minute')
        elif now - last_verify >= VERIFY:
            try:
                got = bmc('get')
                if got != level:
                    log(f'BMC offset was changed to {got} outside the guard; putting back {level}')
                    bmc('set', level)
                last_verify = now
            except Exception as ex:
                log(f'BMC check failed: {ex!r}')
        with open(STATE, 'w') as f:
            json.dump({'time': time.strftime('%F %T'), 'offset': level, **t, 'threads': threads, 'pkg_w': pkg}, f)
        time.sleep(INTERVAL)

if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
